.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt MV 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm mv
.Nd move files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f | i | n
.Op Fl hv
.Ar source target
.Nm
.Op Fl f | i | n
.Op Fl v
.Ar source ... directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
In the first form
.Nm
renames
.Ar source
to
.Ar target .
In the second, the last operand is an existing directory and each
.Ar source
is moved into it under its own name.
.Pp
A
.Ar target
that exists is replaced.
Unless
.Fl f
is given,
.Nm
first asks, on the standard error, when the target can't be written and the
standard input is a terminal, showing the target's mode, owner and group;
an answer beginning with
.Ql y
goes ahead.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f
Replace targets without asking.
.It Fl h
If
.Ar target
is a symbolic link to a directory, replace the link rather than moving
.Ar source
into the directory.
.It Fl i
Ask before replacing any existing target.
.It Fl n
Never replace an existing target.
.It Fl v
Name each file as it is moved.
.El
.Pp
Of
.Fl f ,
.Fl i
and
.Fl n ,
the last given counts.
.Pp
A move within a file system is a
.Xr rename 2 .
Between file systems,
.Nm
copies and then removes: a regular file is copied with its mode, owner,
group and times, anything else as
.Ql cp -PRp
would copy it, after removing any
.Ar target .
The source is removed only once all of it was copied.
A mount point is never moved.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A target left alone by
.Fl n ,
or by answering no, is not a failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Rename a file, asking before replacing anything:
.Dl $ mv -i draft.txt final.txt
.Pp
Move several files into a directory, naming each:
.Dl $ mv -v *.log /var/log/old/
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cp 1 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr symlink 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
.Fl h ,
.Fl n
and
.Fl v
are extensions, as in the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
